The Tragedy of Romeo and the Hundred Numbers.

Romeo, a young man who counts as far as a hundred.
Juliet, a lady who says whatever she is made of.

                    Act I: The Counting.

                    Scene I: The Prologue.

[Enter Romeo and Juliet]

Juliet: You are nothing.

                    Scene II: The Reckoning.

Juliet: Thou art the sum of thyself and a cat.

Romeo: You are the remainder of the quotient between myself and the sum of a
       fair sweet golden cat and the sum of a fair sweet cat and the sum of a
       fair cat and a cat.
Romeo: Are you as good as nothing?
Romeo: If so, let us proceed to Scene IV.

Romeo: You are the remainder of the quotient between myself and the sum of a
       fair cat and a cat.
Romeo: Are you as good as nothing?
Romeo: If so, let us proceed to Scene V.

Romeo: You are the remainder of the quotient between myself and the sum of a
       fair sweet cat and a cat.
Romeo: Are you as good as nothing?
Romeo: If so, let us proceed to Scene VI.

                    Scene III: The Plain Number.

Juliet: Open your heart.
Juliet: Let us proceed to Scene VII.

                    Scene IV: Both Curses At Once.

Romeo: You are the sum of a fair sweet golden noble happy pretty cat and the
       sum of a fair sweet cat and a fair cat.
Romeo: Speak your mind.
Romeo: You are the sum of a fair sweet golden noble happy pretty cat and the
       sum of a fair sweet golden noble happy cat and the sum of a fair sweet
       golden cat and a cat.
Romeo: Speak your mind.
Romeo: You are the sum of a fair sweet golden noble happy pretty cat and the
